{# WHAT THIS INSTALLATION GIVES YOU — the section's first screen, and the
   page the front door's welcome used to be.

   `/` IS THE ORGANIZATION DASHBOARD NOW, and what a fresh installation used
   to be told there had to keep a home somebody can come back to. This is it
   (ruled 2026-09-20).

   EVERGREEN, NOT A FIRST RUN, and every row is built for it: the checklist at
   the bottom never says whether the installation has BEGUN, it says where it
   has got to — "4 registered", "18 of 22 posted", "1 of 4 areas running" —
   which is as worth reading in year three as in week one. A page that was
   true once is a page everybody stops opening, and then the one place that
   says what the platform gives you is a page nobody reads.

   NOTHING ON IT IS TYPED. The figures, the parts of the core, the modules and
   every step arrive from whoever owns the fact; a hand-written list of what
   the platform gives you is wrong the first time it grows a part, on the one
   screen whose whole job is to report.

   "THE GROUND" IS READ FROM THE MANIFESTS, AND THAT IS THE RULING (2026-09-20,
   truth over hand-typed rows). The design draws five named rows — Areas, Team,
   Files, Widgets, Settings — and this card instead lists the parts of the core
   this kernel actually boots, each quoting its own composer.json. So the rows
   are the installation's rather than the drawing's, and they are right on the
   day a part is added, renamed or left out. What is lost is the design's exact
   five words; what is gained is that the card cannot lie. #}
{% extends '@Shell/settings/_section.html.twig' %}

{% block shell_page %}
    {{ include('@Shell/settings/_figures.html.twig', {figures: settings.figures}, with_context: false) }}

    <h2 class="zone">What this installation gives you</h2>

    <div class="grid">
        <div class="c">
            <span class="tab">The ground<span class="src">&middot; here whether a module is installed or not</span></span>
            {# THE CORE'S OWN PARTS, each quoting its own manifest. None carries
               a version: they ride with the core, and a version beside each
               would invite somebody to update one alone. #}
            <table class="tbl">
                {%- for part in settings.coreParts -%}
                    <tr>
                        <td><b>{{ part.name }}</b></td>
                        <td>{{ part.description }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {%- else -%}
                    <tr><td class="fog">Composer cannot say what this core is made of from here.</td></tr>
                {%- endfor -%}
            </table>
        </div>

        <div class="c">
            <span class="tab">What a module adds<span class="src">&middot; installed once, switched on per area</span></span>
            <a class="more" href="{{ modulesUrl }}">Modules &rarr;</a>
            <table class="tbl">
                {%- for module in settings.modules -%}
                    {%- set running = settings.matrix.areasRunning(module.slug) -%}
                    <tr>
                        <td><b>{{ module.label }}</b></td>
                        <td>{{ module.description ?? '—' }}</td>
                        <td class="{{ running ? 'g' : 'fog' }}">
                            {{- running ? 'on in ' ~ running ~ ' ' ~ (running == 1 ? 'area' : 'areas') : 'on nowhere' -}}
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                {%- endfor -%}
                {# THE LAST ROW IS THE OPEN END OF THE LIST, and it is the point
                   of the card: what is installed is not what CAN be. #}
                <tr>
                    <td class="fog">anything else</td>
                    <td class="fog"><span class="mono">composer require</span> a module and its pages, its cells on the
                        dashboard and its rows in the permission matrix arrive with it</td>
                    <td class="fog">&mdash;</td>
                </tr>
            </table>
        </div>

        <div class="c">
            <span class="tab">What stays true<span class="src">&middot; the rules the rest follows</span></span>
            {# THREE STANDING FACTS, in the queue's own row, because they are
               the kind of thing a reader acts on once and then relies on. They
               name nothing installed and go out of date with nothing. #}
            <span class="ao-att watch">
                <span class="sev"></span>
                <span class="bd"><span class="t"><b>A module registers with the registry and renders in the shell.</b>
                    Nothing is wired by hand and no list here is typed — install or remove a package and the pages
                    follow.</span></span>
            </span>
            <span class="ao-att watch">
                <span class="sev"></span>
                <span class="bd"><span class="t"><b>Nothing is granted by default, read included.</b>
                    A person may do exactly what the position they hold grants, where their seat says.</span></span>
            </span>
            <span class="ao-att watch">
                <span class="sev"></span>
                <span class="bd"><span class="t"><b>Zero of everything is a working installation.</b>
                    An empty page here is an honest report, never a half-finished one.</span></span>
            </span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="zone">Next steps</h2>

    <div class="c">
        <span class="tab">Next steps<span class="src">&middot;
            {%- if settings.steps is empty %} nothing installed here publishes one
            {%- else %} the same {{ settings.steps|length }} on day one and on day one thousand &middot;
                {{ settings.stepsDone }} with nothing outstanding{% endif -%}
        </span></span>
        {%- if settings.steps is empty -%}
            <p class="use">A step arrives with whoever owns it — adding an area is one bundle's, composing a
                position is another's. Nothing installed here publishes one yet.</p>
        {%- else -%}
            <table class="tbl">
                <tr>
                    <th>step</th>
                    <th>what it gives</th>
                    <th>where this installation is</th>
                    <th>state</th>
                </tr>
                {%- for step in settings.steps -%}
                    <tr>
                        <td><b>{{ step.label }}</b></td>
                        <td>{{ step.gives }}</td>
                        <td>{% if step.url %}<a href="{{ step.url }}">{{ step.standing }}</a>{% else %}{{ step.standing }}{% endif %}</td>
                        <td class="{{ step.done ? 'g' : 'w' }}">{{ step.done ? 'done' : (step.remaining ?? 'to do') }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {%- endfor -%}
            </table>
        {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {{ include('@Shell/_page_hint.html.twig', {
        lead: 'This is the page to come back to.',
        say: 'It says what the installation gives you and what is left to set up, at any age. Settings is the organization’s, not a module’s: a module keeps its own settings on its own Configure page, and what lives here is what is true of the whole installation.',
    }, with_context: false) }}
{% endblock %}
